Check stdout for errors when an applet returns.
When an applet's main function returned, run_applet_no_and_exit()
exited and left the C runtime to flush stdout, which ignores errors.
Output still buffered at that point, which is all of it unless it fills
the buffer, could fail to be written without any sign. To a pipe whose
reader had exited the status was the applet's own, usually 0, rather
than 141, because the runtime's write bypasses the check in winansi.c;
other errors, such as a closed descriptor or a full disk, were ignored.
factor, ps, df, sum, comm, which, uptime, pgrep and many other applets
were affected. Flush stdout with fflush_stdout_and_exit() instead, so a
broken pipe gives status 141 and any other error a message and the
applet's error status.

---
 libbb/appletlib.c |    6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

--- a/libbb/appletlib.c
+++ b/libbb/appletlib.c
@@ -1236,10 +1236,16 @@
 #  else
 	argc = string_array_len(argv);
 #  endif
+#  if ENABLE_PLATFORM_MINGW32
+	/* The C runtime flushes stdout at exit without checking for errors,
+	 * and a broken pipe found then isn't seen by winansi.c: check here. */
+	fflush_stdout_and_exit(applet_main[applet_no](argc, argv));
+#  else
 	xfunc_error_retval = applet_main[applet_no](argc, argv);
 
 	/* Note: applet_main() may also not return (die on a xfunc or such) */
 	xfunc_die();
+#  endif
 }
 # endif /* NUM_APPLETS > 0 */
 
